The excerpts cover parts of this, but not the full setup. To show a custom field value on the Storefront product detail page, extend the description template and render the field with Twig, for example:

```twig
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/page/product-detail/description.html.twig' %}

{% block page_product_detail_description_content_text %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {# Insert your custom field here, as seen below: #}
    {{ "customFields.my_test_field"|trans|sw_sanitize }}: {{ page.product.translated.customFields.my_test_field }}
{% endblock %}
```

The template file goes in `<plugin root>/src/Resources/views/storefront/page/product-detail/description.html.twig` [1]. The sample uses `my_test_field`, so replace it with your field name, which here would be the material field [1].

To let merchants fill in the field in the Administration, add it to the product's detail page with a plugin: create `main.js` in `<plugin root>/src/Resources/app/administration/src`, and use `Shopware.Component.override('sw-product-settings-form', { template })` with a Twig template extension [6].

The excerpts do not show how to define the custom field itself (its entity set, name, or type configuration), so you will need the custom field guide for that [4]. Also, the excerpts do not describe how a text custom field is created in the Administration with a plugin, so that step is not covered here.

You can create a scheduled task by extending the `ScheduledTask` class and implementing `getTaskName` and `getDefaultInterval`, where the interval is in seconds, so 10 minutes would be `600` [1]. The task's handler extends `ScheduledTaskHandler`, is annotated with `#[AsMessageHandler(handles: ExampleTask::class)]`, and puts its logic in `run()` [1].

```php
class ExampleTask extends ScheduledTask
{
    public static function getTaskName(): string
    {
        return 'swag.example_task';
    }

    public static function getDefaultInterval(): int
    {
        return 600;
    }
}
```
[1]

The documentation provided does not describe how to read a plugin settings checkbox from within a scheduled task handler, so the conditional check (only doing work when the checkbox is enabled) needs to be implemented based on other plugin configuration documentation not included here. You would place that check at the start of `run()` [1].

To register the task without reinstalling the plugin, use `bin/console scheduled-task:register` [6]. To test it, run `bin/console scheduled-task:run` and then `bin/console messenger:consume`, making sure the task's `status` is `scheduled` in the `scheduled_task` table [6]. You can also run a single task directly with `bin/console scheduled-task:run-single <task-name>` [5].

Define the message as a serializable PHP object that contains the data your handler needs. The excerpts show `SmsNotification` as the message class, but not its definition, so you will need to create it yourself following the message description [5]. Create a handler class and mark it with the `#[AsMessageHandler]` attribute and an `__invoke` method that takes the message. Put your slow SMS work inside `__invoke` [2]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/MessageQueue/Handler/SmsHandler.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\MessageQueue\Handler;

use Symfony\Component\Messenger\Attribute\AsMessageHandler;
use Swag\BasicExample\MessageQueue\Message\SmsNotification;

#[AsMessageHandler]
class SmsHandler
{
    public function __invoke(SmsNotification $message)
    {
        //... do some work - like sending an SMS message!
    }
}
```

To put the job on the queue, inject `Symfony\Component\Messenger\MessageBusInterface` into a service and dispatch the message on the bus. The default bus is `messenger.default_bus` [3]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Service/ExampleSender.php
public function sendMessage(string $message): void
{
    $this->bus->dispatch(new SmsNotification($message));
}
```

The excerpts do not show how to start a consumer for processing on a production system beyond noting that the queue should be processed via the CLI and that `messenger:consume` workers are recommended, managed by a process control system such as systemd or supervisor [4]. The exact command setup for your environment is not covered in the provided documentation.

Create a Storefront controller that extends `StorefrontController` and declares the storefront route scope on the class: `#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StorefrontRouteScope::ID]])]` [4]. Each action needs a `#[Route]` attribute with an HTTP method and a return type [4].

To make the route cacheable, set the default option `_httpCache` to `true` on the route attribute [1]. Only `GET` requests are considered cacheable [2].

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Storefront/Controller/ExampleController.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Storefront\Controller;

use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Shopware\Storefront\Controller\StorefrontController;
use Shopware\Storefront\Framework\Routing\StorefrontRouteScope;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Attribute\Route;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StorefrontRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleController extends StorefrontController
{

    #[Route(path: '/example', name: 'frontend.example.example', methods: ['GET'], defaults: ['_httpCache' => true])]
    public function showExample(): Response
    {
        return $this->renderStorefront('@SwagBasicExample/storefront/page/example/index.html.twig', [
            'example' => 'Hello world'
        ]);
    }
}
```
[1]

With this setup, if the route is called several times in the same state, a response is generated only for the first request and later requests get the same response [1]. Shopware distinguishes the user states `logged-in` and `cart-filled`, and the cache reacts to them [1]. Cache invalidation of the controller route is controlled by the Store API route invalidation, as long as the controller loads its data via Store API routes [3].

The excerpts do not show how to create the plugin or register the route beyond this example, and they do not describe the `frontend` route name requirement in detail beyond the general guideline [4].

The excerpts cover only part of this. A menu entry is what opens your module [3], and the excerpts do not show the full steps for registering a menu entry. Creating the module itself and adding a menu entry are covered in the linked guides [2]. To restrict access, use the privilege identifier, which is the key plus the role, for example product.viewer [5]. Add privilege to the meta property of your route to protect its pages:

```javascript
Module.register('your-plugin-module', {
    routes: {
        detail: {
            component: 'your-plugin-detail',
            path: 'your-plugin',
            meta: {
                privilege: 'your_key.your_role' // e.g. 'product.viewer'
            }
        }    
    }
});
```
[6]

To hide the menu entry, add privilege to the navigation settings:

```javascript
Module.register('your-plugin-module', {
    navigation: [{
        id: 'your-plugin',
...,
        privilege: 'your_key.your_role' // e.g. product.viewer
    }]
});
```
[6]

For a settings item, add privilege in the same way:

```javascript
Module.register('your-plugin-module', {
    settingsItem: [{
        group: 'system',
        to: 'sw.your.plugin.detail',
        privilege: 'your_key.your_role' // e.g. product.viewer
    }]
});
```
[6]

The excerpts do not show how to register your own privilege, so the role must already exist. The guide for adding permissions covers this, but its registration steps are not included here [1]. Also note that ACL rules in the Administration can be bypassed with direct API calls to the backend, so protect the backend data too [1].

The excerpts show how to dispatch the event and how to listen to it, but not how to write the event class itself. The event class is referenced as "our event class" without its code [1].

**Dispatching the event:** Inject the `EventDispatcherInterface` into a service and call `dispatch` with your event object. The excerpt's example service is `ExampleEventService`, which is passed `ExampleEntity` and `SalesChannelContext` and dispatches `new ExampleEvent($exampleEntity, $context)` [1]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Service/ExampleEventService.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Service;

use Shopware\Core\System\SalesChannel\SalesChannelContext;
use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\Event\ExampleEvent;
use Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example\ExampleEntity;
use Symfony\Contracts\EventDispatcher\EventDispatcherInterface;

class ExampleEventService
{
    private EventDispatcherInterface $eventDispatcher;

    public function __construct(EventDispatcherInterface $eventDispatcher)
    {
        $this->eventDispatcher = $eventDispatcher;
    }

    public function fireEvent(ExampleEntity $exampleEntity, SalesChannelContext $context)
    {
        $this->eventDispatcher->dispatch(new ExampleEvent($exampleEntity, $context));
    }
}
```

**Listening to the event:** Create a subscriber class that implements `EventSubscriberInterface`. Its `getSubscribedEvents()` method maps the event to a method name [3]. The excerpt's example uses a Shopware event constant, but it does not show a constant for a custom event, so use your event's name in that position [3]. The excerpts do not show the exact name constant for `ExampleEvent`:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Subscriber/MySubscriber.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber;

use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;

class MySubscriber implements EventSubscriberInterface
{
    public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
    {
        return [
            // <your event name> => 'onExampleEvent'
        ];
    }

    public function onExampleEvent(/* event object */)
    {
        // Do something
    }
}
```

The subscriber must also be registered in the dependency injection container in `services.php` and tagged with `kernel.event_subscriber`. Without this registration, Shopware will not call it, and there may be no obvious error [3].

**Missing from the documentation:** the event class definition, the event name constant, and the `services.php` registration syntax are not covered by the excerpts.
